  "worked_solution": "The parametric equations of an ellipse centered at $(h,k)$ with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ are\n\n$$\nx=a\\operatorname{cos}\u2061\\theta +h,\\,y=b\\operatorname{sin}\u2061\\theta +k,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nIn our case, the ellipse is centered at the point $(-2,1),$ with $a=4$ and $b=5.$ Therefore, the parametric equations of the *full* ellipse are\n\n$$\nx=4\\operatorname{cos}\u2061\\theta -2,\\,y=5\\operatorname{sin}\u2061\\theta +1,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nTo restrict the parameterization to a quarter-ellipse, we again interpret $\\theta $ as the central angle measured from the horizontal line through the center $(h,k).$ On the quarter-arc where both $\\operatorname{cos}\u2061\\theta \\ge 0$ and $\\operatorname{sin}\u2061\\theta \\ge 0$ (moving from the rightmost point to the topmost point), the parameter must satisfy $\\theta \\in [0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}].$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14004891/assets/df6c12657a6a487f93ec3a6213be8ea16e68d363001bdf0ddb8ff68b5201a8c4.png)\n\nWe can see that if we restrict $\\theta $ to $[0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}]$, we get the quarter-ellipse.\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations of the quarter-ellipse are \n\n$$\nx=4\\operatorname{cos}\u2061\\theta -2,\\,y=5\\operatorname{sin}\u2061\\theta +1,\\,\\theta \\in [0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}].\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
